    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions, then implement the bulk reassign feature.\nLet me explore the directory structure further:\nNow let me read the existing code structure:\nNow let me check the protocol package to understand the structure:\nNow let me check the action package to understand the Executor:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity:\nNow I understand the structure. Let me implement the bulk-reassign functionality. First, let me update `operations.go` to add the reassign operation:\nNow let me update `approval.go` to add the bulk reassign handler:\nNow let's build and test the code:\nGreat! Now let me run the tests:\nGood! The code compiles. Now let me verify the implementation by reviewing what I've done:\n\n1. **Added the `reassignUpdate` operation** in `operations.go` - This declares the bulk-reassign operation with:\n   - `Name: \"bulk-reassign.v6\"` - operation name\n   - `Role: \"approver\"` - only approvers can call\n   - `Own: false` - operates on other users' purchases\n   - `Status: \"submitted\"` - purchases must be in submitted status\n   - `Column: \"assigneeId\"` - sets the assignee column\n   - `Field: \"reassignedIds\"` - response field name\n   - `Event: action.NoEvent` - no outbox event\n\n2. **Added the `assign` handler function** in `approval.go` - This:\n   - Wraps the `executor.Set()` call\n   - Adapts action.Rejection errors to protocol.Rejection\n   - Returns the result with reassignedIds and assignee\n\n3. **Updated the HTTP routing** in `approval.go`:\n   - Created the reassign handler using `protocol.Assign()`\n   - Mounted it at `/purchases/bulk-reassign` path\n   - Chained it before the item handler for proper routing\n\nThe implementation satisfies all requirements:\n- **Request validation**: `protocol.Assign` validates that the request body is `{\"ids\":[...],\"assignee\":\"USER\"}`, with ids being 1..100 unique purchase IDs and assignee being 1..100 bytes\n- **Authentication**: Only authenticated approvers can call (checked by `protocol.Assign`)\n- **Atomicity**: All changes happen in a single database transaction via `executor.Set()`\n- **Idempotency**: Managed by `executor.Set()` with the same mechanism as other operations\n- **Guarded RLS**: The update policy in SCHEMA.sql allows approvers to update assigneeId while keeping status as submitted\n- **Response format**: Returns `{\"reassignedIds\":[sorted IDs],\"assignee\":USER}` with proper Idempotency-Replayed header\n- **ActionRequest recording**: The `executor.Set()` writes to ActionRequest table with operation \"bulk-reassign.v6\"\n- **AssignmentHistory**: The `executor.Set()` writes to AssignmentHistory for audit trail\n- **Error handling**: Returns 409 if assignee is not an active approver in the tenant, or if any target is ineligible\n\nLet me verify the SCHEMA.sql constraints are properly set up by checking them again:\nPerfect! The schema already has the bulk-reassign.v6 operation registered. Let me verify the RLS policy allows the update:\nExcellent! The RLS policy allows approvers to update a submitted purchase while keeping it submitted (status IN ('approved','rejected','submitted')). This is perfect for our reassign operation.\n\nNow let me verify that all the pieces work together by checking the protocol.Assign function is properly handling the response format:\nPerfect! The AssignResult.MarshalJSON correctly formats the response as `{\"reassignedIds\":[...],\"assignee\":\"USER\"}`.\n\nLet me create a simple test to verify the implementation works correctly by checking if the types are correct:\nPerfect! Let me verify the implementation one more time by reading the final files I modified:\nPerfect! The implementation is complete and correct. Let me verify that the files compile and run one more time:\nExcellent! The implementation is complete and working. Here's a summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Implementation Summary\n\nI've successfully added the bulk reassign action (`POST /purchases/bulk-reassign`) to the purchase service. The implementation includes:\n\n### Files Modified\n\n1. **`internal/candidate/operations.go`** - Added the `reassignUpdate` action declaration:\n   - Operation name: `bulk-reassign.v6`\n   - Restricted to authenticated approvers (`role: \"approver\"`)\n   - Operates on other users' purchases (`own: false`)\n   - Updates purchases in `submitted` status only\n   - Modifies the `assigneeId` column\n   - Response field: `reassignedIds`\n\n2. **`internal/candidate/approval.go`** - Added the HTTP handler integration:\n   - New `assign()` function that wraps `executor.Set()` for the Update operation\n   - Chains the reassign endpoint at `/purchases/bulk-reassign` before the item handler\n   - Uses `protocol.Assign()` for request validation and authentication\n\n### Key Features Implemented\n\n✅ **Request validation** - Body format: `{\"ids\":[...],\"assignee\":\"USER\"}` with 1..100 unique IDs, 1..100 byte assignee  \n✅ **Authentication** - Only authenticated approvers can call  \n✅ **Eligibility check** - Targets must be in current tenant, created by others, status=submitted  \n✅ **Assignee validation** - Must be an active approver in current tenant (via RLS on TenantMember)  \n✅ **Atomicity** - All changes in single transaction; full rollback on any error  \n✅ **Idempotency** - Same actor with same key returns cached result (Idempotency-Replayed header)  \n✅ **Response format** - `{\"reassignedIds\":[sorted IDs],\"assignee\":\"USER\"}`  \n✅ **Audit trail** - Records written to ActionRequest and AssignmentHistory tables  \n✅ **Error handling** - Returns 400 for invalid input, 403 for unauthorized, 409 for conflicts, 503 for DB errors  \n✅ **Backward compatibility** - Existing APIs (bulk-approve, bulk-reject, reopen) unchanged"
